{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>This is the Torah: a man when he dies.</b> The verse's saying <b>זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה</b> may also be explained along the lines of what I wrote in the verse ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״ — namely, because He was precise to say <b>אָדָם כִּי יָמוּת</b> etc., and our Sages of blessed memory (Bava Metzia 114a) expounded: ״You are called אָדָם — Israel defile in a tent, but the nations of the world״ do not, etc. Therefore He prefaced by giving the reason that surrounds the matter and said <b>זֹאת הַתּוֹרָה</b> — whose cause is that this one who is called אָדָם defiles in a tent, but the nations of the world, who do not have the Torah, etc. And see what I have written in the verse ״זֹאת חֻקַּת הַתּוֹרָה״." "<b>All who come into, etc., and all that is in the tent.</b> It is difficult: if the one who comes in is impure, what does ״all that is in the tent״ lack compared to him? And it may be explained based on what they said in Pesikta Zutarti, and this is their language: ״כָּל הַבָּא אֶל הָאֹהֶל״ — who comes in with part of his body; end quote. According to this, if the verse had said only ״כָּל הַבָּא,״ I would say: one who comes in entirely — from where do I know one who comes in with part of his body? And from the fact that it says ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר בָּאֹהֶל,״ behold the entire thing is stated. What then do I fulfill by ״כָּל הַבָּא״ etc.? That is one who comes in with part of his body. Further, there in the Pesikta, this is their language: Another matter — ״כָּל הַבָּא״ etc. — to make the floor of the house like it [i.e., subject to impurity] down to the abyss; end quote. It appears that they were compelled [to derive] this interpretation, for according to the first reasoning the verse should have said ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר בָּאֹהֶל וְכָל הַבָּא אֶל הָאֹהֶל,״ and the verse would be explained in the manner of ״not only this but also that.״ But from the fact that it prefaced ״כָּל הַבָּא,״ this indicates that the statement of <b>כָּל אֲשֶׁר</b> etc. is a new law." "<b>However,</b> in the Sifrei I saw both approaches: the first approach is that of Tanna Kamma, and the second is that of Rav Aḥa bar Yoshiya. And Tanna Kamma concludes that the reason the verse needed to say ״כָּל אֲשֶׁר בָּאֹהֶל״ even though it already said ״כָּל הַבָּא״ — which means coming in with part of his body — is because of the principle that punishments are not derived by inference. And it is difficult for me: is not what we call ״punishments are not derived by inference״ concerned with a death penalty administered by an earthly court, whereas here, if one became impure and entered the Sanctuary while impure — his death is at the hand of Heaven? And besides this, when we reflect on the matter, it appears that what is before us is not called ״punishments derived by inference,״ for within one who comes in entirely there is also coming in with part of his body, and he is thus liable for the partial entry as well, since every single part of him is called ״part of his body,״ and we are not punishing him by inference but from the verse itself. Perhaps this Tanna holds that everything of this nature is called ״punishments derived by inference.״"], :book :numbers, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:11:16.739845155Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}